#COMMENTS: The same target were used for both the time-of-flight
#and spectrometer methods. Targets were 2 or 3-cm diameter
#cylinders made of natural material.
#
#Method: Time-of-flight for low energy neutrons, < 400 MeV
#Pulse-shape discriminationFor neutron-gamma discrimination.
#High energy measurements, >200 MeV, were done using
#(N,P) scattering on a liquid hydrogen converter and
#reconstruction of the proton trajectory in a rotating
#magnetic spectrometer.
#
#
#1.Estimations of systematic errors in the time-of-flight method
#error-(%) 2-20-MeV 20-100-MeV 100-400-MeV
#subtraction of
#accidental 5.8 5.8 5.8
#coincidences
#gamma rejection 2.9 2.9 2.9
#efficiency
#determination 10. 4. 10.
#Total error 11.9 7.6 11.9
#
#2.Estimations of systematic errors as a function of incident energy
#error (%) 0.8-1.2-GeV 1.6-GeV
#beam monitoring <5.8 <5.8
#spectrometer response
#function <4 <11.5
#unfolding procedure <5.8 <8.6
#total uncertainty <9.1 <15.5


